## Bug description

The `pre-read` hook warns against (and flags in stats as) "repeated reads" when a file has already been read in the current session — but it never checks whether the file was *modified* since the last read. This means:

| Scenario                                                                                 | What happens               | What should happen                           |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Claude reads `foo.ts` → **Claude writes to `foo.ts`** → Claude reads `foo.ts` again      | ❌ Warned as repeated read | Re-read is legitimate — file content changed |
| Claude reads `foo.ts` → **user edits `foo.ts` externally** → Claude reads `foo.ts` again | ❌ Warned as repeated read | Re-read is legitimate — file content changed |
| Claude reads `foo.ts` → nothing changed → Claude reads `foo.ts` again                    | ✅ Warned (correct)        | Correct                                      |

This inflates the `repeated_reads_blocked` and `estimated_savings_vs_bare_cli` statistics, since some of those "blocked" re-reads were actually necessary.

## Root cause

In `src/hooks/pre-read.ts`, the session state (`_session.json`) records only:

```ts
session.files_read[normalizedFile] = {
    count: 1,
    tokens: 0,
    first_read: new Date().toISOString(),
};
```

It never records the file's `mtime` at the time of read, and the re-read check never compares against current `mtime`. The write hooks (`pre-write.ts` / `post-write.ts`) also never clear the `files_read` entry for a file that was written.

## Suggested fix

Track `read_mtime` in the session entry and compare against current `mtime` before warning:

```ts
// In pre-read.ts, when recording first read:
import { statSync } from "node:fs";

session.files_read[normalizedFile] = {
    count: 1,
    tokens: 0,
    first_read: new Date().toISOString(),
    read_mtime: statSync(normalizedFile).mtimeMs,  // ← ADD
};

// When checking for repeated reads, compare mtime:
if (session.files_read[normalizedFile]) {
    const prev = session.files_read[normalizedFile];
    const currentMtime = statSync(normalizedFile).mtimeMs;
    if (currentMtime <= prev.read_mtime) {
        // File has NOT changed — warn safely
        process.stderr.write(`⚡ ... already read this session (~${prev.tokens} tokens). Consider using your existing knowledge.\n`);
        session.repeated_reads_warned++;
    } else {
        // File WAS modified (by Claude or user) — re-read is legitimate, reset entry
        delete session.files_read[normalizedFile];
        // Fall through and re-register below
    }
}
```

Alternative / complementary approach: have `post-write.ts` clear the `files_read` entry for any file that was written (when the path is recoverable from the write hook input).

## Environment

- OpenWolf version: v1.0.4
- Node.js 20+

